# Report figure: Mauna Loa annual mean CO2, 1960–2025, with linear and quadratic fits

This directory is the `figtab-repo` delivery (work assignment, `report/figures/`):
the report's single figure and its generator. The report text (`report/report.md`)
is `paper-repo`'s; the data and the fits are `experiment-repo`'s.

## Files

- `co2_mlo_fits.png` — the figure, ~300 dpi raster, the file `report.md` links.
- `co2_mlo_fits.pdf` — the same figure as vector, kept alongside.
- `make_figure.py` — the generator (see below for reproducing).
- `README.md` — this contract.

## Inputs and exactness

- Data: `experiment-repo`'s canonical download, `data/co2_annmean_mlo.csv` with
  `data/provenance.json` (URL, fetch time 2026-10-20T01:17:51 UTC, SHA-256
  bdd956e8d7e05ebc3c61ac1f8f8c8cea4a505df56724fba5200b4eb1e0089b2b, last year
  2025). The generator verifies the CSV's SHA-256 against that record, and the
  handoff's recorded input hash, before plotting — the plotted points are the
  committed download.
- Fits: `experiment-repo`'s analysis handoff, `analysis/results.json`
  (`figtab-fits-handoff-1` schema, at its main c13de75; the assembled delivery
  binds their `data/` only, so the handoff is read from their repository).
  66 annual means, 1960–2025, x = year − 1992.5. Linear: slope 1.686 ppm/yr.
  Quadratic: 357.073 + 1.686x + 0.013401x². Window rates (average quadratic
  derivative over the window's annual labels = derivative at the midpoint):
  1960s 0.935 ppm/yr, 95% CI [0.901, 0.969]; 2016–2025 2.436 ppm/yr, 95% CI
  [2.402, 2.470]. The figure prints these values verbatim from the handoff
  (3-decimal display) and never recomputes them; the curves are evaluated from
  the handoff's coefficients and match its `annual_fits.csv` predictions to
  1e-13 ppm.

## Layout constraints (paper-repo's answer on the report slot)

Rendered for the report's half-page slot: 6.5 in wide x 3.2 in tall (full text
width, low height), fonts >= 8 pt at final size (ticks 8, legend 8, annotations
8.2); fits distinguishable in grayscale as well as color (dashed grey line vs
solid red line vs dark markers); the PNG is the file `report.md` embeds, the PDF
rides along. The figure carries axis labels, the legend, the two ten-year
windows and their growth rates — no provenance text (the report body carries
URL, fetch time, SHA-256, and the caption) and no fit-quality annotation (per
the analysis owner's answer); the conditional nature of the conventional
intervals and the residual serial correlation are stated in the report, not the
figure.

## What the figure shows, and why

The claim it makes checkable: observed annual means rising, and rising faster
in the last ten years than in the 1960s, with the reported numbers readable
off the plot.

- Year on the x-axis, CO2 annual mean (ppm) on the y-axis, per the field's
  convention (NOAA GML's own annual-mean trend plots).
- Observed 1960–2025 annual means as dark markers; the OLS line as a grey
  dashed line (the constant-growth baseline, its average slope in the legend);
  the OLS quadratic as a red solid line.
- The two ten-year windows lightly shaded, each with a tangent segment at the
  window midpoint (1964.5 / 2020.5) whose slope is the handoff's stated rate,
  so the two rates can be seen, not just read.
- Each rate annotated verbatim with its 95% interval, exactly as committed.

Layout is asserted at render time: the generator refuses to save a figure
whose legend or annotations would overlap the observed points or the fitted
curves. The rendered outputs were inspected programmatically (page size, verbatim
strings, nothing off-page, curve agreement with the handoff predictions, pixel
size, both fit colors present); the inspection harness lives in `work/`.

## Regenerating

```sh
python3 report/figures/make_figure.py \
  --data <co2_annmean_mlo.csv> --provenance <provenance.json> \
  --fits <results.json> --outdir report/figures
```

Requires matplotlib + numpy. Defaults point at the assembled-delivery layout;
override `--data/--provenance/--fits` for local runs (the data and handoff are
`experiment-repo`'s files).
